RM(1)                         Funny CLI Manual                         RM(1)

NAME
    rm, del — Delete a file permanently, because "Are you sure?"
              would apparently slow things down.

SYNOPSIS
    rm <file>
    del <file>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'rm' command deletes the specified file from the filesystem.

    There is no confirmation prompt.

    There is no recycle bin.

    There is only you, your decision, and the consequences.

    Directories are deliberately rejected. If you want to remove a
    directory, the command politely suggests using 'rmdir' instead.

ALIASES
    del
        An alias for 'rm'.

        For those who instinctively type 'del' because they've spent
        too much time somewhere else.

ARGUMENTS
    file
        The file to delete.

        Required. If omitted, 'rm' refuses to guess what you want
        destroyed.

ERRORS
    No such file: <path>
        The specified file does not exist.

        Excellent. Nothing was deleted. Crisis averted.

    Use rmdir for directories.
        You attempted to delete a directory with 'rm'.

        The command has standards.

    rm error: <error>
        Something prevented deletion.

        Permissions, filesystem problems, or perhaps the universe
        intervened at the last possible moment.

OUTPUT
    On successful deletion:

        Deleted <path>

    A small message confirming that the file has ceased to exist.

EXAMPLES
    > rm old-notes.txt
        Deleted old-notes.txt

        One less file. One more opportunity for regret.

    > del old-notes.txt
        Same thing, different dialect.

    > rm mystery.log
        Deleted mystery.log

        If it contained something important, you should have named it
        something less mysterious.

    > rm .
        If '.' resolves to a directory, the command refuses.

        Nice try.

    > rm important.txt
        Deleted important.txt

        There is no undo command.

        You knew the risks.

FUN FACTS
    - 'rm' only deletes files, not directories.
    - Hidden files are not protected by special magic. If you name one,
      it can be deleted like any other file.
    - There is no '-i' confirmation option.
    - There is no trash can.
    - 'del' exists for people whose muscle memory comes from a different
      command-line universe.
    - The command prints "Deleted" only after the filesystem agrees.

